{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} computer window; windows; system window; disk drives; start menu; system properties; dialog boxes; properties}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote 
\pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Computer
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par This window contains the disk drives which have been detected, plus some useful programs.  When you close the computer window, Calmira will close down.  
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc computer.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Settings dialogs
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The computer window is the centre of Calmira, and if you right click between the icons, a popup menu appears, which lets you access dialogs to configure the program.  These dialog boxes 
are often available from other places but you can always open them from here.  
\par 
\par When you right click on the Computer icon, the Start Menu appears (it is normally accessed from the Taskbar).  You can make the Computer's normal popup menu appear instead, through the {\uldb System Properties dialog}{\v SystemProp}.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} IconWindows}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Icon windows}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} icon windows; icons; windows; files; folders; explorer; large icons; small icons; list view; file system properties; properties}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Icon windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 {\*\bkmkstart UserGuide}{\*\bkmkend UserGuide}
\par These windows provide many facilities for managing your files and programs.  Each window displays the contents of one folder, and each folder may only be shown by one window.  If you try and open a folder which is already displayed, the existing window is
 restored and brought to the front, as required.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc iconwin.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par If you right click over an icon, the {\i object menu} appears.  Commands in the top half affect all selected objects, those in the bottom half work on the focused object.  If you right click in the space between icons or the grey window border, the {\i 
window menu} appears.  These commands affect the icon window or the folder it represents.
\par 
\par To open the parent folder, press Backspace.  To close or arrange groups of windows, see the {\uldb keyboard commands}{\v KeyCommands} summary.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Alternative viewing modes
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 When there is not enough room on the status bar for two panels, only one panel is displayed, showing different information depending on whether you have selected any files.  You can still access the m
ouse operations of the right hand label, by shift-clicking on the single label.
\par 
\par The window menu lets you choose between large icons, small icons and list view.  Note that the small icons view is just a squashed version of the large icons view -- the icons are arranged in rows, not in columns as expected!
\par 
\par The Set Filter menu command lets you change the contents shown in the current window.  To set defaults for all new windows, use the {\uldb File System Properties}{\v FileSystemProp}
 dialog.  From here, you can also choose whether to draw miniature icons for each file instead of using blank file icons.
\par  
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Managing files and folders
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 First {\uldb select}{\v SelectingObjects}
 the objects which you want to work with.  To delete, rename or describe them, pick the appropriate command from the object menu.  To copy, drag the icons to the destination (either a window or another icon).  If you right-click during the drag, the opera
tion is toggled between a copy and a move.
\par 
\par {\b See also:}{\b 
\par }{\uldb File Management}{\v Fil}{\v eManagement}
\par {\uldb Explorer}{\v Explorer}
\par {\uldb Shortcuts and aliases}{\v References}
\par {\uldb Wastepaper bin}{\v Bin}
\par {\uldb File System Properties}{\v FileSystemProp}
\par 
\par \pard \s5\fi-720\li720 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Explorer}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $} Explorer}} 
{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} explorer; tree; disk drives; folders}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Explorer
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par This displays all drives and folders in a branching structure similar to File Manager.  Use it to give a broad view of your file system while you navigate through the folders.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc explorer.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Win95 Explorer style view
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
To combine the tree with an icon window for an interface like the Windows 95 Explorer, right click on the tree and check the "Show file window" menu item.  Now when you click on a node of the tree, the icon window is updated to display the contents of tha
t folder.
\par 
\par 
\par {\b See also:}{\b \line }{\uldb Icon windows}{\v IconWindows}
\par {\uldb Desktop Properties}{\v DesktopProp}
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Bin}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $} Wastepaper Bin}} 
{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} bin; trash; emptying the bin; restoring files; bin directories; locations; drives; [Bin Locations]}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 
\f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Wastepaper Bin
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par When you drop files or folders into the bin icon, they are moved to hidden directories instead of being deleted.  If you change your mind, you can restore them to their original location.  Otherwise, you'll need to empty the bin to free up the space used 
by the files.
\par 
\par Double click on the icon to open the bin window.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc bin.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 How to restore files
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 After selecting the items, choose Restore from the popup menu to move each file or folder back to where it came from.  Alternatively, drag the items to an icon window or shortcut.  This moves them all to that location.

\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 How to empty the bin
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 The popup menu has Delete and Empty commands, which deletes selected items and all trash, respectively.  If you choose {\uldb Properties}{\v BinProp} from the menu, you can configure the bin to clear trash automatically.

\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Using more than one bin directory
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can specify which drives to store trash on, and which folders to use.  If you have more than one hard disk d
rive, this could speed things up because trash from each drive can be moved quickly and need not be copied to another disk.
\par 
\par In the [Bin Locations] section, add mappings of drive=folder.  For example:
\par 
\par {\f5 [Bin locations]}{\f5 
\par }{\f5 c=c:}{\f5 \\}{\f5 windows}{\f5 \\}{\f5 temp}{\f5 
\par }{\f5 d=d:}{\f5 \\}{\f5 waste}{\f5 
\par }{\f5 f=e:}{\f5 \\}{\f5 trash}{\f5 
\par }
\par This tells Calmira to store all files from drive F: in the folder e:\\Trash.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Automatically binning deleted files
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can redirect all files deleted from Calmira's icon windows into the bin by selecting the "Move objects into bin instead of deleting" option from the {\uldb File System Properties dialog}{\v FileSystemProp}
.  When you press Del from an icon window, the effect is the same as dropping the objects into the bin icon.
\par 
\par This setting does not affect files deleted by other programs, or from the Find Files dialog or Explorer tree.  You must manually empty the bin to free up disk space, but remember that files could be stored on different drives so you need to check the [Bin
 locations] section if you need more space on a particular disk.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Taskbar}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $} Taskbar}} 
{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} taskbar; applet tray; terminating a task; excluding programs; clock; time; programs}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 
\f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Taskbar
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par Each running process has a button on the bar and you click on a button to switch to that task.  If you right click instead, a popup menu lets you minimize, maximize or close the window.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc taskbar.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par The start button has a special popup menu that lets you setup the Taskbar.  Choose {\uldb Taskbar Properties}{\v TaskbarProp} from this menu to adjust the settings.  
\par 
\par You can configure the Taskbar to always stay on screen or hide when the cursor moves off it.  It will always pop up when the cursor reaches the bottom of the screen.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 The Applet Tray
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
This is located on the Taskbar's clock, and it can hold a few icons for commonly used utilities.  These programs are treated slightly differently from those launched by normal shortcuts.  Firstly, Calmira doesn't run a new copy of the program if it is alr
eady running.  Secondly, the applets are not shown on the Taskbar, since they can be brought forward from the Applet Tray.  You can add and remove applets from the Taskbar Properties dialog
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Excluding programs
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
There are some programs which you may not wish to appear Taskbar.  For example, programs which have icons that change appearance, like the Windows Clock, should stay as an icon on the desktop. Also, space on the Taskbar is valuable, so you should not show
 a Taskbar button for programs you don't often switch to.  Use the Exclusion tab page in the Taskbar Properties dialog to specify which programs to exclude.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Terminating a task
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 This feature is useful for killing off a stubborn program that won't go away, assuming it has not already crashed Windows.
\par 
\par 1.  Choose Terminate from the Start button's popup menu.
\par 2.  The cursor should change to a circle with a bar through it.
\par 3.  Left click on the button of the offending program.
\par 
\par To abort the termination instead, right click anywhere.
\par 
\par {\b\cf6 Warning}: ending programs this way will not free all the resources and memory it has used, and could lead to the system becoming unstable.  You should consider restarting Windows after saving any files.
\par 
\par 
\par {\b See also:}{\b 
\par }{\uldb Start Menu}{\v StartMenu}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} StartMenu}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $} Start menu}}
 {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} start menu; menu; properties}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Start Menu
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par This nested menu is displayed when you press Start on the Taskbar. Each menu item is equivalent to a program item in Program Manager.  Additionally, it can open n
ew windows and dialogs, and programs that are launched from the Start Menu will be shown by your chosen icon on the Taskbar, even Windows programs.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc start.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par To edit the Start Menu, right click on the Start button and choose {\uldb Start Properties}{\v StartMenuProp} from the popup menu.  From there, you can also copy your program groups and icons from Program Manager.
\par 
\par The {\uldb System Properties dialog}{\v SystemProp} lets you choose whether to open the Start Menu when you right click over the {\uldb Computer}{\v Computer} icon.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} References}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Shortcuts and aliases}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} shortcuts; aliases; references; icons; desktop; properties; als; programs; files; folders}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote 
\pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Shortcuts and Aliases
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par Shortcuts and aliases are references -- they point to another object such as a disk drive, folder, or file.  When you open or drop things into a reference, the effect is the same as if you had opened or dropped into the original object.  You can also crea
te a link to an {\uldb Internet}{\v Internet} address.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Shortcuts
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 These are icons on the desktop, and you can create them in two ways: either drag an icon out of a window and release the mouse, or right click on the {\uldb desktop}{\v Desktop}
 and choose an item from the New Shortcut submenu.
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc shortcut.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par The shortcut is saved in Calmira's INI file when you quit.  To edit its properties, click on its caption and choose {\uldb Properties}{\v ReferenceProp}
 from the menu.  Shortcuts are effective when used sparingly -- you would probably have shortcuts of your disk drives and a few important folders and utilities on your desktop.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Aliases
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
These are small files (with an extension ALS) containing an icon and some data that points to the original object. To create aliases of visible objects, drag them to another folder or window, and hold down the Shift key before releasing the mouse.  To cre
ate an alias of something not currently visible, right click between the icons and choose Create Alias from the window menu. 
\par 
\par \pard \s5\qc \{bmc alias.bmp\}
\par \pard \s5 
\par Aliases are just like other files, and you can copy, move or delete them without affecting the original object.  To change their properties, right click and select the Edit Alias command.
\par 
\par You can use aliases to give the effect of a free floating program group: move aliases into the one folder, create a shortcut of the folder, and change its icon.  They are also useful for creating "virtual links" to other folders, to change the apparent st
ructure of your directory tree.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Desktop}}{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057  }{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 
\f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $} Desktop}}{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057  }{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} d
esktop; menu; icons; arranging icons; shortcuts; cascading windows; minimizing programs; repaint; properties}}{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057  }{\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}}{\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057  }Desktop
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par If you right click on your wallpaper, the desktop menu appears, which gives you fast access to frequently used features.  There are facilities to let you create a shortcut at the point where you clicked, and arrange desktop icons and windows.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Activating Calmira using the desktop
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 When Calmira is hidden behind other windows, you can bring it forward by right clicking on the desktop while holding down the left mouse button.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Opening and creating folders
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
The Open Folder command is particularly useful because it lets you quickly get to any directory without travelling through the tree.  To see only certain files, add a wildcard (which must include ? or *) after the folder name.  For example c:\\Windows\\
System\\*.dll.
\par 
\par To create a new folder, select this menu command, type in a new folder name, and you will be asked whether it should be created.  The new folder is opened for you in a blank window.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Differences between Open Folder and Run dialog
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 You can open an icon window from the {\uldb Run Program dialog}{\v RunProgram}
 by typing in a folder name, which gives you the benefit of a history list.  However, the Open Folder command is a little more powerful because it can create new folders, and you don't need to type in the full pathname -- if you omit a drive, Calmira uses
 the drive on which Windows is installed; if you omit a leading backslash, Calmira adds it for you.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Creating a new folder on the desktop
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 Use the desktop menu to create a folder shortcut.  In the {\uldb Shortcut Properties dialog}{\v ReferenceProp}
, enter a caption and the name of a new directory, then press OK.  Calmira will ask you whether you wish to create a new directory.
\par 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} FileManagement}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 File Management}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} file management; files; folders; copying; moving; dragging; drag and drop; deleting; renaming; refreshing windows}} {\plain 
\f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} File Management
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Copying
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Ensure the destination is visible, as a window or an icon.
\par 2.  Select which objects to copy.
\par 3.  Drag them to the destination and release the mouse.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Moving
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Ensure the destination is visible, as a window or an icon.
\par 2.  Select which objects to move.
\par 3.  Drag them to the destination.
\par 4.  Right click {\i once}.
\par 5.  Release the mouse.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Deleting
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Select which objects to delete.
\par 2.  Right click over one of the selected icons.
\par 3.  Choose Delete from the popup menu.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Renaming
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Select an object to rename.
\par 2.  Right click over the icon.
\par 3.  Choose Rename from the popup menu.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Creating a folder
\par \pard\plain \s6\keep \f2\fs20\lang2057 1.  Open the parent folder as an icon window.
\par 2.  Right click in the space between the icons.
\par 3.  Select Create Folder from the menu.
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Refreshing windows
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 When you have used another program to change files or folders, existing icon windows must be refreshed to show the correct contents.  Press F5 or click on the left label on the status bar to read the directory again.

\par 
\par 
\par {\b See also:}{\b 
\par }{\uldb Working with descriptions}{\v Descriptions}
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s2\keepn \b\f2\cf2\lang2057 \page {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 #} Descriptions}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 ${\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 $}
 Working with descriptions}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 K{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 {\fs16\up6 K} descriptions; 4DOS; captions; filenames; descript.ion}} {\plain \f3\fs16\up6\lang2057 +{\footnote \pard\plain \s245 \f3\fs20\lang2057 
{\fs16\up6 +} 00000}} Working with descriptions
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
\par In the absence of long filenames in Windows 3.x, Calmira allows you to use 4DOS compatible  descriptions to label your files and folders.  When you upgrade to Windows 95 or Windows NT, you can turn the descriptions into proper long filenames.
\par 
\par Descriptions can either show up as popup hints (and an extra column in the list view), or be used as captions for the icons.  The {\uldb File System Properties}{\v FileSystemProp} dialog lets you adjust the settings.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Adding and changing descriptions
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 After enabling the descri
ptions feature, open an icon window and select the files you want to describe.  Right click over one of them and choose the Describe command.  Calmira will prompt you for a description for each file.  Press Escape or the Cancel button to stop adding descr
iptions and save the descriptions you have accepted.
\par 
\par To delete descriptions, simply describe the files with empty strings.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 Running 4DOS sessions simultaneously
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
Calmira keeps a copy of the descriptions in memory for each icon window, but if you use a 4DOS box to manage files, it may modify the descriptions so that the Calmira's memory copy is out of date.  When you next use Calmira to copy files, the old descript
ions are carried across.
\par 
\par To prevent this, select the option to maintain up to date descriptions.  This causes descriptions to be read from disk {\i before} each update, to refresh the copy in memory.  There is still a risk in multitasking environments that co
nflicting updates will occur, so you are advised not to use two programs to modify the same directory at once.  Closing all icon windows will make it safe to modify the directory from the 4DOS prompt.
\par 
\par \pard\plain \s3\sa30 \b\f2\fs20\cf1\lang2057 How descriptions are stored
\par \pard\plain \s5 \f2\fs20\lang2057 
When a directory contains a file or folder that has a description, a hidden file called Descript.ion is created which stores the extra data.  When files are copied, moved or deleted, the description file is updated.  When folders are copied or moved, desc
ription files are handled automatically without asking for confirmation, even though they have the hidden attribute.
\par 
\par 
\par {\b\cf6 Warning}: Calmira 2.0 doesn't handle descriptions properly when you {\i merge} whole directory structures together \endash 
 the description files are merged on the assumption that all files are replaced, regardless of whether the file operations succeed.
\par 
